<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Sui</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.) · capital Sui (Suizhou)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Ji-clan state in Hubei, now identified with the Zeng of the bronzes, whose Marquis Yi was buried with the most famous set of chime bells in China.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> was founded early in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou</a> by a branch of the royal Ji clan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suizhou">Suizhou</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>, guarding the corridor to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. Excavated bronzes call the state Zeng, and most scholars now treat Sui and Zeng as the same state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the 8th and 7th centuries BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> led the small states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> region against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-chu">King Wu of Chu</a> died on campaign against it in 690 BCE. It later became a close Chu ally and sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-zhao-of-chu">King Zhao of Chu</a> when Wu armies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ying-chu">Ying</a> in 506 BCE. The tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-yi-of-zeng">Marquis Yi of Zeng</a>, buried around 433 BCE, held 65 bronze bells, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">lacquerware</a> and early writing on bamboo.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> faded from the record and was absorbed by Chu, probably in the 4th century BCE. The excavation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-yi-of-zeng">Marquis Yi</a>'s tomb in 1978 transformed knowledge of early Chinese music and court life.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-sui.jpg" alt="State of Sui" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:States_of_Zhou_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1046 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sui (Suizhou)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Marquis Yi of Zeng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zeng, Sui (Zhou state)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-723&amp;focus=state-of-sui" aria-label="State of Sui on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7601 1023 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Sui lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7921 1279L7909 1324"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7909 1324h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7921 1279h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7921 1279h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-723&amp;focus=state-of-sui">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

